WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
Configuration is part of release management.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:09.400
Development, test, staging, and production should have clearly separated values and credentials.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:17.260
Production configuration should not be copied into local development or broad test environments unless there is a documented, controlled need.

4
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:24.400
A code change and a configuration change may be deployed together, but only one may be visible in a pull request.

5
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:30.660
That is why high-risk configuration changes need review, approval, logging, and rollback planning.

6
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:40.620
CI/CD systems may hold powerful deployment credentials, registry tokens, cloud permissions, secret-management access, and environment variables.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:48.320
Avoid exposing production configuration to untrusted pull requests, forks, preview builds, or broad build jobs.

8
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:56.100
Scope variables and credentials to the workflow, branch, environment, repository, or deployment target that needs them.

9
00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:01.800
Build jobs that only install dependencies should not automatically inherit production deployment secrets.

10
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:08.080
Containers and serverless platforms make configuration delivery convenient, but they can also hide drift.

11
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:12.580
Avoid baking secrets into images or build artifacts.

12
00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:20.360
Promotion between environments should make configuration differences visible rather than relying on undocumented console settings.

13
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.620
Rollback plans should account for both code and configuration changes.

14
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:32.960
If a release fails because of a setting, operators need to know whether to roll back code, revert configuration, rotate a secret, or all three.
